# `<Product>` — the spec

Copy this to `docs/workflow/SPEC.md` at the end of discovery and fill it in.

One document, written once and read at the start of every build session. It is
the handover between the phase that decides what to build and the phase that
builds it — what is in, what is out, in what order, and what has already been
decided so that nobody decides it a second time differently.

**It is committed**, unlike `STATE.md`. `STATE.md` is where the work got to and
changes every session; this is what the work _is_, and a change to it deserves a
diff and a reason. When the two disagree, this one is right and `STATE.md` is
stale.

Replace every `<…>`. A section left as a placeholder is a section the build phase
will improvise, and improvised scope is how a cut feature comes back.

```
Product:     <name>
Written:     <date>
Author:      <who ran discovery>
Supersedes:  <a previous version of this file, or —>
```

## 1. Who this is for

`<A job title, a company size, and what their week looks like. Specific enough
that you could show it to somebody and they could say "no, that is not us".>`

Not a persona. If it would fit on a poster it is too wide, and a spec that opens
with "teams who want to move faster" has not begun.

## 2. What they do instead today

`<The incumbent: a spreadsheet, an inbox rule, a contractor, a competitor, or
nothing at all. Name it, and name what it costs them — in money if you know, in
hours if you do not.>`

**Their price is your ceiling:** `<£x a month, or the hours a week it eats>`.

Every product competes with something, and "nothing" is a real answer that
usually means the pain is not felt yet. If you cannot name the incumbent, you do
not yet know what you are replacing.

## 3. The riskiest assumption

> `<One sentence. The thing that, if false, makes the rest of this pointless.>`

**Status:** `<unproven | proven by <what>, on <date> | disproven — stop>`

Carried forward from discovery verbatim. It is almost never "can we build it",
and the build phase's job is to reach the moment it can be tested rather than to
finish the list. A spec that loses this builds the wrong thing exactly on
schedule.

## 4. What this does

`<One paragraph, present tense, addressed to the person in §1. No technology in
it — if the paragraph needs a noun from this repository to make sense, it is
describing the implementation rather than the product.>`

## 5. The slices

Each row is a **vertical slice**: schema, handler, screen and tests, working end
to end. Not "the backend for X" — half a thing cannot be demonstrated and cannot
be cut, which are the only two things you will want to do with it.

Rows are in dependency order and none may depend on one below it. Build takes the
topmost row that is not `landed`, and does not start the next until the whole
gate passes — `pnpm check && pnpm lint && pnpm test`, plus `pnpm e2e` when the
slice touched a route or a handler.

| #   | Slice                          | Module                | Tenant-owned | Shown by                               | State |
| --- | ------------------------------ | --------------------- | ------------ | -------------------------------------- | ----- |
| 1   | `<what a person can now do>`   | `<name>` / existing   | yes / no     | `<the test that fails if it breaks>`   | todo  |
| 2   | …                              | …                     | …            | …                                      | todo  |

`State` is one of `todo`, `building`, `landed`.

**Tenant-owned is not a formality.** _Yes_ commits you to a row-level security
policy in the style of `0002_rls.sql` **and** `withOrgScope` around every query —
both, never either — plus a case in `e2e/tests/tenancy.spec.ts`. _No_ is a
declaration that every organization sees the same rows, which is sometimes right
and should be somebody's decision rather than an omission.

**Shown by is a test, a screen or a curl**, named. "It works" is not a
demonstration, and a slice whose column is empty is a slice nobody can tell is
finished.

How many rows fit: [01-discover.md](01-discover.md#how-much-fits) has the
measured cost of the modules in this repository, which is better evidence than
anybody's estimate.

## 6. The data

New tables only. What the starter already carries — users, organizations,
members, roles, permissions, API keys, the audit log, and the rest — is not
listed here.

| Table      | Owned by              | Why it is not a column on something that exists |
| ---------- | --------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| `<name>`   | organization / global | `<reason>`                                      |

The third column earns its place: most new tables should have been a column, and
writing the reason down is what catches the ones that should.

## 7. Not in this, and why

As important as §5, and the section people actually reopen. Write the reason and
not just the item — "cut for time" expires the moment there is time, whereas "the
first ten customers do this by hand and that is fine" still holds in a year.

- `<item>` — `<why>`

When it will not fit, cut in this order: a second frontend or anything
cross-tenant, then configurability, then integrations beyond the first, then the
dashboard. **Never cut** tenant isolation, the audit trail, or the tests that
prove both — they are what a security review asks about, and retrofitting them
costs more than building them did.

## 8. Decisions already taken

The ones that would otherwise be argued twice, with the reason attached so the
argument does not need a third round.

| Decision   | Because    | Taken by | On       |
| ---------- | ---------- | -------- | -------- |
| `<what>`   | `<reason>` | `<who>`  | `<date>` |

A decision with no reason recorded is one somebody will reverse in good faith.

## 9. Open questions

Each with a name against it and the date after which answering it costs rework. A
question with neither is a note, and belongs somewhere else.

| Question   | Blocks   | Needed by | Who      |
| ---------- | -------- | --------- | -------- |
| `<what>`   | slice #n | `<date>`  | `<name>` |

